# Runbook: @@title@@

What to do while the product runs. Hosting (Forge Web) does the routine work; this page says what
it does and what is left for you.

## Is it up?

- `GET /healthz` answers `{"status": "ok"}` while the server runs and the database answers.
  Hosting checks it before and after every release and rolls a release back when it fails.
- The go-live page in Forge Web shows each release, its checks and its health.

## Logs

The server logs to standard output; Forge Web shows the logs per service. Mail sent without a
relay (local runs) appears in the log, too. Never log passwords, tokens or full request bodies.

## Backups and restore

- Hosting dumps the database (`pg_dump`) before every migration and every night, encrypted and
  off the host.
- To restore, pick a backup in Forge Web. Locally: `pg_restore --clean --dbname "$DATABASE_URL"
  backup.dump`, then `uv run alembic upgrade head` if the backup is older than the code.

## Releases and rollbacks

- A release is a commit that passed `forge app check`, the release review and your approval.
- Migrations run once, before the new release takes traffic. Write them so the previous release
  still works on the new schema (`docs/migrations.md`); then a rollback needs no down-migration.

## Secrets

Secrets (names in `forge.app.toml`, values in Forge Web) reach the server as environment
variables. To rotate one, set the new value in Forge Web and release again; the old value stops
working when the release switches.

## Abuse and content reports

- Notices arrive in `/api/moderation/reports` (moderators only). Decide every one: remove the
  content or dismiss the notice, always with a statement of reasons; the reporter gets it by mail.
- Make someone a moderator in the database: `UPDATE users SET is_moderator = true WHERE email =
  '…';`
- Illegal content must be handled quickly; keep the decisions (the `reports` table does).

## Requests from users

- **Export:** users download their data themselves (`GET /api/account/export`).
- **Deletion:** users delete their account themselves (`DELETE /api/account`); their rows go with
  it, their reports stay without their name.
- If someone asks by mail instead, do the same for them and answer within one month (GDPR).

## Too many requests

Each client address may make 300 API requests and 10 sign-in, sign-up or reset requests per
minute (`server/app/settings.py`); more get `429` with `Retry-After`.
